WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:04.550
Along with behind-the-scenes features and
the occasional Marvel One-Shot, the gag reels

00:00:04.550 --> 00:00:06.960
are a favorite staple of Marvel Blu-ray releases.

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:10.880
Fans not only get to laugh, but we also see
the bonds formed behind the scenes.

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:13.680
And it's hard not to fall in love with them
just a little bit.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:17.680
Here are some of the most hilarious bloopers
and gags waiting on the Thor: Ragnarok Blu-ray.

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:19.560
Butterfingers

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:24.350
One of Mjolnir's amazing properties is that
when Thor wills it back to his hand, it comes

00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:25.020
straight away.

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:29.980
Even if the hammer needs to cross nations,
galaxies, or Doctor Strange's entire collection

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:33.020
of sorcerer artifacts, Mjolnir always comes
home.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:40.320
"Sorry."

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:47.340
Unfortunately, Mjolnir's fantastic abilities
are one thing, and the properties of Thor:

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:48.480
Ragnarok's props are another.

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:52.380
The Blu-ray's gag reel proves that either
someone off-camera wasn't very good at throwing,

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:54.440
or the cast just isn't very good at catching.

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:01.140
This kind of thing is to be expected.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:04.580
By all appearances, Chris Helssworth has never
been able to catch anything anyway.

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:06.740
Low blows

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:10.740
Thor: Ragnarok included some fantastic leading
female characters.

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:15.660
Cate Blanchett turned in a fun and utterly
vicious portrayal of the lead villain, and

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:19.120
Tessa Thompson was funny, believable, and
badass as Valkyrie.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:20.580
"He's mine"

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:31.380
And that just makes the number of crotch shots
that happened during filming even more fitting,

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:36.220
especially since almost all of them come unintentionally
from Blanchett, Thompson, or Thompson's stunt

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:36.720
double.

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:41.040
"Are you Okay?"

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:44.020
Pew pew!

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:48.840
You may be familiar with the story that, while
filming lightsaber duels in the Star Wars

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:52.360
prequels, Ewan McGregor had to make an effort
not to make his own lightsaber sounds.

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:54.780
"To begin with it was quite difficult not
to make the sound."

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:55.340
"Yeah."

00:01:55.380 --> 00:02:00.660
"Bzzzz and also when it goes away… it makes
that nice sshhhhhhh noise when it goes down."

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:03.180
Well apparently, it's not as uncommon as you'd
think.

00:02:03.409 --> 00:02:07.700
The Ragnarok gag reel shows us a number of
shots when Kate Blanchett had to stop because

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:10.300
she realized she was making comic-book sound
effects.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:16.540
"I shouldn't make the noises with my mouth
should I?"

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:19.620
Warm up

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:22.000
Not everything on the gag reel is a blooper.

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:26.240
Sometimes, perfectly reasonable things like
warming up between takes just looks funny.

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:32.800
"Hey!"

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:35.620
They're not playing to the camera or making
any funny mistakes.

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:39.210
They're just making sure they don't pull a
hamstring when they pretend to be super people

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:40.120
in a couple of minutes.

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:41.220
No harm in that.

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:43.340
All fall down

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:48.100
Maybe it's a side effect of the Revengers'
escape from Sakaar through the "Devil's Anus."

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:53.030
Or maybe director Taika Waititi took the bottomless
mug of beer from the Doctor Strange scene

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:55.100
and passed it around the cast and crew every
day.

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:59.950
But for whatever reason, no one in the cast
of Thor: Ragnarok seemed to be able to stay

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:01.960
upright for more than a few seconds at a time.

00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:11.920
What makes their various spills that much
funnier is that it never seems like there's

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:13.780
anything in particular knocking them down.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:16.780
They don't seem to be in the middle of complicated
action sequences.

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:18.300
They just fall down.

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:19.740
A lot.

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:24.120
"f---"

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:26.360
Avenging bros

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:31.340
While Chris Hemsworth and Mark Ruffalo don't
have the same chemistry going as Iron Man

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:35.870
and Hulk did in The Avengers, they've got
their own buddy feel in Thor: Ragnarok, though

00:03:35.870 --> 00:03:37.790
it's definitely more Odd Couple.

00:03:37.790 --> 00:03:39.140
"I smashed you."

00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:40.280
"Yeah, sure sure"

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:40.860
"Baby arms."

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:41.700
What

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:42.220
"Baby."

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:44.600
"You're a big child."

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:45.340
"Thor go."

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:46.280
"I am going!"

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:53.640
Almost two years before the film's release,
Mark Ruffalo compared the Thor/Hulk relationship

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:57.700
in Ragnarok to the one between Robert De Niro
and Charles Grodin in 1988's Midnight Run.

00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:03.000
It's a love/hate relationship, but the two
still share some hilarious moments in the

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:03.500
gag reel.

00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:08.180
"I thought you were gone forever I'm glad
to see you back!"

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:10.060
"You're ruining my disguise!"

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:11.540
Unique skill

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:15.280
The Grandmaster of the comics isn't a particularly
fun guy.

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:19.740
Unless you think it's fun to habitually kidnap
superheroes to make them fight for your amusement.

00:04:19.930 --> 00:04:24.420
Regardless, Jeff Goldblum's realization of
the character is one of the best reasons to

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:25.360
see Ragnarok.

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:28.720
"You're officially pardoned...from life"

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:37.840
And when he shows up in the gag reel, he definitely
leaves an impression, most notably when he

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:39.840
demonstrates his real life super power:

00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:40.960
"Watch this."

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:45.460
Dancing fools

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:50.080
Most of the Marvel gag reels feature bits
of the cast breaking into dance while filming,

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:52.460
and the reel for Thor: Ragnarok is no exception.

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:57.560
But while the cast occasionally cut a rug,
the fanciest feet on the set undoubtedly belonged

00:04:57.560 --> 00:05:01.030
to director Taika Waititi, who apparently
never stops dancing.

00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:06.620
"A day on the set working with Taika involves
hijinks, dancing, Yeah, he's just a big kid."

00:05:06.700 --> 00:05:11.460
In the end, all that play time translated
into an incredibly fun movie, so maybe Taika

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:12.620
Waititi is onto something.

00:05:13.180 --> 00:05:15.020
"That is how you make movies."

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:16.900
Thanks for watching!

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:19.580
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:21.720
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

